"el": "Ευγένεια, ευχές και επιφωνήματα",
"title": "Politeness, wishes and exclamations",
"span": "A2–B1",
"blurb": "The grammar of being polite and sociable: εσύ or εσείς, softening a request (θα ήθελα, μήπως θα μπορούσατε), addressing people (κύριε Νίκο, κυρία Παππά, θεία), pet names, the wish for every occasion (Χρόνια πολλά, Περαστικά, Καλορίζικο, Με γεια) and its answer, suggestions and refusals, signs and prohibitions, and exclamations (Τι ωραία που είναι! Πόσο μεγάλωσες! Να τος! Για έλα!).",
"intro": [
{
"p": "Greek social life runs on set phrases. There is a wish for a new haircut, a new house, a name day, the first day of the month and the end of an illness, and each has an expected answer. Getting them right matters more for how people feel about you than any verb ending. This chapter also covers the grammar of politeness (the plural of respect, softened requests) and the exclamations that make speech sound natural."
},
